ormal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>One problem=
 with the fact that the Trump administration wants to step up the economic =
war against Iran=3F It sends a signal that the U.S. doesn=E2=80=99t particu=
larly want to go back to actually fighting Iran, as my colleague Anton Troi=
anovski, a global affairs correspondent, has pointed out.</span></p><p styl=
e=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>=
Trump=E2=80=99s reluctance to go back to war won=E2=80=99t come as a surpri=
se to anyone who has watched the conflict and seen the president issue dire=
 warnings on several occasions about the destruction he=E2=80=99s about to =
unleash =E2=80=94 only to walk them back.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal=
 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Trump has a leve=
rage problem. The war is unpopular, the U.S. has elections coming up and he=
 doesn=E2=80=99t want the war to be central to the campaign. Iran knows thi=
s, and wants to use it.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia=
,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Over the weekend, Iran=E2=80=99s s=
ecurity chief issued a warning that any country that joined U.S. efforts to=
 isolate Iran would be =E2=80=9Cregarded by us as an enemy.=E2=80=9D Should=
 the economic pressure ramp up, one analyst told Anton, Iran is likely to l=
ook for a way to put fresh military pressure on the U.S. or its Gulf allies=
=2E</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;ma=
rgin:0 0 15px"><span>But it also might not have to. Bessent=E2=80=99s annou=
ncement followed a familiar pattern. He=E2=80=99d spent days promising an =
=E2=80=9Ceconomic D-Day,=E2=80=9D the sanctions equivalent of storming the =
beaches of Normandy. But when the announcement finally came, as one of our =
reporters put it, it was more like a warning shot.</span></p><table border=
